[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The cross-domain “translator” in the middle (r11) is healthy and exchanging routes between the OSPF core (20.x side) and the EIGRP edge (120.x side). End-to-end tests show no outage: pc0 (20.1.1.10) and pc12 (120.1.1.10) reach each other with 0% loss and ~1 ms RTT. On r11, OSPF neighbors are up (Full to 111.1.1.1 on eth1/B), and EIGRP adjacencies are established to r12 on 112.1.1.2 (eth0/EE). r11’s OSPF is configured to “redistribute eigrp,” and r11’s routing table contains 120.0.0.0/8 learned via EIGRP; r1 (OSPF side) also learns 120.0.0.0/8 via OSPF from r11, confirming the EIGRP→OSPF leak is active. On the EIGRP side, r12 has a default route via r11 (default via 112.1.1.1), which provides reachability back to 20.x; this is corroborated by the successful pc12→pc0 ping. 

Localization: r11 is the protocol boundary—OSPF on eth1 (111.x/B) toward r1 and EIGRP on eth0 (112.x/EE) toward r12. Mechanism: mutual connectivity is achieved by redistributing EIGRP into OSPF on r11 and using a default toward r11 on the EIGRP side.

Root cause: Not reproducible—post-maintenance, the translator is operational and both sides are exchanging routes.

Corrective fix: None required. If the design goal is symmetric route exchange without reliance on a default, consider adding “redistribute ospf” under EIGRP on r11; current connectivity, however, is fully functional as verified. 
=== END ===